.TH XUR.STD.DECIMAL 7 "2026-11-22" "xur 0.1.3" "Xur Manual"
.SH NAME
xur.std.decimal \- arbitrary-precision decimal numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
use std::decimal;

let d = decimal::from_int(100);
let e = decimal::from_str("3.14")?;
io::println("{}", d);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B std::decimal
provides a
.B Decimal
type backed by the
.B rust_decimal
crate, giving 96 bits of significand with a scale of 0\(en28.
.PP
Decimals avoid the rounding issues of floating-point arithmetic and are
the native representation of .NET
.B System.Decimal
values, making them essential for interacting with CoreCLR methods that
take or return decimals.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.B from_int(value) -> Decimal
Create a decimal from an integer.
.TP
.B from_float(value) -> Result<Decimal, str>
Create a decimal from a float. Returns an error if the float
cannot be represented exactly (e.g. NaN, infinity).
.TP
.B from_str(value) -> Result<Decimal, str>
Parse a decimal from a string such as
.BR \(dq3.14\(dq " or " \(dq\-100.5\(dq .
.SH METHODS
.TP
.B scale() -> int
The number of decimal places (0\(en28).
.TP
.B is_sign_negative() -> bool
True if the value is negative.
.TP
.B is_sign_positive() -> bool
True if the value is positive or zero.
.TP
.B abs() -> Decimal
Absolute value.
.TP
.B floor() -> Decimal
Round towards negative infinity.
.TP
.B ceil() -> Decimal
Round towards positive infinity.
.TP
.B round() -> Decimal
Round to the nearest integer (half away from zero).
.TP
.B round_dp(dp) -> Decimal
Round to
.I dp
decimal places.
.TP
.B to_int() -> int
Convert to an integer, truncating towards zero. Returns 0 if the
value is out of range.
.TP
.B to_float() -> float
Convert to a 64-bit float. Returns 0.0 if the value is out of range.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
use std::decimal;
use std::io;

fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let price = decimal::from_str("19.99")?;
    let qty = decimal::from_int(3);

    io::println("price = {}", price);
    io::println("rounded = {}", price.round());
    io::println("scale = {}", price.scale());

    Ok(())
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xur.std (7),
.BR xur.std.num (7)
